1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:05,470
One timber comes down across
another, at right angles.

2
00:00:06,360 --> 00:00:11,590
Both have been cut, so the two
settle into each other and interlock.

3
00:00:11,967 --> 00:00:15,917
This is the watari-ago, a crossing joint.

4
00:00:21,367 --> 00:00:26,857
In episode five, a splice
joined timbers end to end.

5
00:00:27,607 --> 00:00:31,507
The other family, shiguchi,
joins timbers that meet

6
00:00:31,547 --> 00:00:35,817
at an angle, and the
watari-ago is one of them.

7
00:00:36,100 --> 00:00:40,690
As an everyday word,
watari can mean a crossing.

8
00:00:41,820 --> 00:00:50,590
The second kanji here is read ago, jaw;
on its own it can also be read era, gills.

9
00:00:51,420 --> 00:00:55,690
The Flat 35 house specification
writes that part in kana.

10
00:00:56,067 --> 00:00:58,537
Which part of the joint is the jaw?

11
00:00:59,467 --> 00:01:02,857
None of our sources
say, so we won't guess.

12
00:01:06,567 --> 00:01:10,217
A Japanese dictionary
describes it like this.

13
00:01:10,587 --> 00:01:14,397
The upper timber gets a
groove across its underside.

14
00:01:15,227 --> 00:01:21,057
The lower timber is cut away left and
right, leaving its middle standing.

15
00:01:21,533 --> 00:01:23,803
The upper timber comes down.

16
00:01:24,493 --> 00:01:27,503
The standing middle
fits up into the groove,

17
00:01:28,013 --> 00:01:30,683
and the cut-away sides take the rest.

18
00:01:31,633 --> 00:01:36,363
In our model, neither timber
can now slide along the other.

19
00:01:36,833 --> 00:01:40,023
In the model, nothing
stops it lifting, though.

20
00:01:40,333 --> 00:01:46,823
Seen from the end of the lower timber,
its top steps down on both sides.

21
00:01:47,513 --> 00:01:51,763
The groove in the timber above
fits over the raised middle.

22
00:01:52,100 --> 00:01:54,410
How deep are the cuts?

23
00:01:54,960 --> 00:01:59,340
No modern specification we
found gives a depth, and an

24
00:01:59,380 --> 00:02:02,710
architecture dictionary
simply calls them shallow.

25
00:02:03,280 --> 00:02:06,210
The depths drawn here are
only for illustration.

26
00:02:10,567 --> 00:02:12,527
It isn't a half-lap.

27
00:02:12,967 --> 00:02:17,627
In a half-lap, aigaki,
each timber is cut to

28
00:02:17,667 --> 00:02:21,477
half its depth, and
their faces finish flush.

29
00:02:21,933 --> 00:02:26,893
In the watari-ago, the cuts
are shallow, the timbers sit at

30
00:02:26,933 --> 00:02:31,243
different heights, and the uncut
middle locks into the groove.

31
00:02:31,833 --> 00:02:34,053
The dictionary notes it's used where two

32
00:02:34,093 --> 00:02:36,983
timbers cross at slightly
different heights.

33
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:42,950
Where is it used?

34
00:02:43,700 --> 00:02:46,660
Where a roof beam meets
the eaves plate, the

35
00:02:46,700 --> 00:02:50,090
Flat 35 specification offers two choices:

36
00:02:50,540 --> 00:02:56,090
a dovetail called kabuto-arikake,
or the watari-ago.

37
00:02:56,880 --> 00:02:59,330
Either one is fastened with a plate bolt.

38
00:02:59,733 --> 00:03:05,653
Where the tops of the two are to finish
flush, it calls for a different joint:

39
00:03:05,693 --> 00:03:10,863
a housed dovetail, which
isn't a crossing joint at all.

40
00:03:11,133 --> 00:03:15,333
The same specification uses
it where second-floor beams

41
00:03:15,373 --> 00:03:20,073
rest on another horizontal
member, and where log roof

42
00:03:20,113 --> 00:03:24,703
beams cross a support,
there held with staples.

43
00:03:25,973 --> 00:03:30,153
And according to a Japanese
architecture glossary, one

44
00:03:30,193 --> 00:03:35,053
traditional framing style, which
sets the roof beam on top of

45
00:03:35,093 --> 00:03:39,623
the eaves plate, uses this
joint or the dovetail there.

46
00:03:43,700 --> 00:03:48,200
Japan's building regulations
require the structurally important

47
00:03:48,240 --> 00:03:54,490
joints of a timber frame to be
fastened, with bolts, staples, pegs

48
00:03:54,880 --> 00:03:58,270
or another approved method,
so they carry their loads.

49
00:03:59,100 --> 00:04:03,280
Depending on where it's used,
the specification pairs this

50
00:04:03,320 --> 00:04:09,010
joint with a plate bolt,
staples, nails or other bolts.

51
00:04:09,433 --> 00:04:14,623
A plate bolt is a steel plate
with a bolt welded to it.

52
00:04:14,953 --> 00:04:18,393
The plate is fixed to
one member, and the bolt

53
00:04:18,433 --> 00:04:22,223
passes through another and
is tightened with a nut.

54
00:04:22,600 --> 00:04:28,800
The regulations also forbid notching
the underside of a beam, or other

55
00:04:28,840 --> 00:04:34,170
horizontal member, near the middle of
its span where that would weaken it.

56
00:04:34,620 --> 00:04:38,200
In the uses above, the joint
is cut where one timber

57
00:04:38,240 --> 00:04:42,010
rests on the other, not
out in the open span.

58
00:04:45,700 --> 00:04:48,690
Next time, we go back to splices.

59
00:04:49,767 --> 00:04:51,017
The watari-ago:

60
00:04:51,567 --> 00:04:55,047
both timbers cut, the
middle locked into the

61
00:04:55,087 --> 00:04:57,997
groove, and hardware to hold it together.
